device_id,device_ip,management_type,iris_vlan,svi_ip,svi_mask,app_ip,app_mask,app_gateway,inband_vlan,ios_ssh_host,model,vpg_number,nat_interface,svi_igp,role,platform
# One row per device — NETWORK INFO ONLY. Tokens and secrets are handled
# automatically by the Console/API onboarding flow (see the Management Type
# and VLAN Ownership doc). Comment lines (#) and the header are ignored on import,
# so EVERY example row below is inert until you uncomment it. Addresses are
# TEST-NET-1 (192.0.2.0/24, RFC 5737) placeholders -- replace them with yours.
#
# management_type (required): 'routed', 'inband', 'router-routed', 'router-nat', or 'xr-host'.
#   routed  = IRIS creates a dedicated VLAN + SVI. Fill iris_vlan, svi_ip,
#             svi_mask, app_ip, app_mask, app_gateway. Leave inband_vlan blank.
#   inband  = attach to an EXISTING operator-owned VLAN. IRIS never creates,
#             changes, or removes that VLAN/SVI/gateway/routes/VRF. Fill
#             inband_vlan, app_ip, app_mask, app_gateway. Leave iris_vlan/svi_* blank.
#   router-routed = IRIS creates a VirtualPortGroup (VPG) app subnet. Fill
#             app_ip, app_mask, app_gateway, and vpg_number; no NAT.
#   router-nat = router-routed plus overload NAT behind nat_interface and static
#             PAT for TCP 6881. NAT cleanup is tracked in the deployment record;
#             a pre-existing `ip nat outside` marking is preserved.
#             The interface is canonicalized during preflight; named globals
#             and guest-share must be collision-free and are tracked the same way.
#   xr-host = IOS-XR appmgr container on the router's own network stack. Leave
#             all VLAN/SVI/app-address/VPG/NAT fields blank; use platform=xr-appmgr.
# 'model' is OPTIONAL (e.g. C9300-48UXM, IE-3400, C8000V); blank lets onboarding detect it.
# 'role' is OPTIONAL. It uses 1-32 lowercase letters, numbers, dot, underscore,
#   or hyphen. A blank role preserves an existing declaration during CSV import;
#   use the role API or iris-role CLI to clear membership deliberately.
# svi_igp: OPTIONAL, routed only. 'isis' adds 'ip router isis' to the IRIS SVI
#   for a fabric that must learn its subnet (e.g. an SD-Access underlay); blank
#   keeps the server's SVI_IGP env var default (none) for that device. Every
#   other management_type must leave it blank.
# 'platform' is LAST: choose 'guestshell', 'iox', 'router', or 'xr-appmgr'. A
#   blank imported value is an inventory transition state; known IOS-XE models
#   can resolve at onboard, while uncertainty and IOS-XR without xr-appmgr fail closed.
#   Inband supports Guest Shell and IOx (IE-3x00, C9300); DHCP is NOT supported.
# ios_ssh_host: OPTIONAL for inband IOx. The app SSHes to the switch's mgmt IP
#   (device_ip) by default; set this only to override it. Blank for Guest Shell.
#
# Routed example (dedicated IRIS VLAN/SVI):
# edge-routed,192.0.2.10,routed,666,192.0.2.9,255.255.255.252,192.0.2.10,255.255.255.252,192.0.2.9,,,C9300-48UXM,,,,,guestshell
# Routed example on an IS-IS fabric (SD-Access underlay learns the IRIS subnet):
# edge-routed-isis,192.0.2.15,routed,667,192.0.2.13,255.255.255.252,192.0.2.14,255.255.255.252,192.0.2.13,,,C9300-48UXM,,,isis,,guestshell
# Inband Guest Shell example (existing management VLAN 120, static address):
# edge-inband,192.0.2.20,inband,,,,192.0.2.21,255.255.255.0,192.0.2.1,120,,C9300-48UXM,,,,,guestshell
# Inband IOx example (IE-3400, app SSHes to the existing management SVI 192.0.2.1):
# ie-inband-iox,192.0.2.30,inband,,,,192.0.2.31,255.255.255.0,192.0.2.1,120,192.0.2.1,IE-3400,,,,,iox
# C8000v router NAT example (VPG 10; use an operator-selected outside interface):
# edge-c8kv,192.0.2.40,router-nat,,,,10.8.0.2,255.255.255.252,10.8.0.1,,,C8000V,10,GigabitEthernet1,,,router
# Cisco 8201 IOS-XR example (no app-network fields):
# edge-xr,192.0.2.50,xr-host,,,,,,,,,8201,,,,,xr-appmgr
